                      %ul
                        %li
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis+and+physics"} Riemann hypothesis and physics
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/physicscontents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#RelationToQuantumOperations"} Relation to superoperators and quantum operations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What has been called
            %em Geometry of Interaction
            (
            %a{:href => "#Girard89"}> Girard 89
            ) is a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            for
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            that is however different in method from the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            of a linear entailment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\vdash B
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            as in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            %em Geometry of Interaction
            interprets it as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphism
            on the object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\multimap B
            %em operational semantics
            to contrast with the traditional
            = succeed "." do
              %em denotational semantics
          %p
            That also the “operational semantics” of GoI has an interpretation in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+categories"} compact closed categories
            induced from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traced+monoidal+categories"} traced monoidal categories
            was first suggested in (
            %a{:href => "#JoyalStreetVerity96"}> Joyal-Street-Verity 96
            ) and then developed in (
            %a{:href => "#Hines97"}> Hines 97
            ,
            %a{:href => "#Haghverdi00"}> Haghverdi 00
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AbramskyHaghverdiScott02"} Abramsky-Haghverdi-Scott 02
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HaghverdiScott05"} Haghverdi-Scott 05
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Shirahata"} Shirahata
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToQuantumOperations Relation to superoperators and quantum operations
          %p
            We discuss a relation of the GoI to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superoperators"} superoperators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operations"} quantum operations
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %p
            According to (
            %a{:href => "#AbramskyHaghverdiScott02"}> Abramsky-Haghverdi-Scott 02, remark 5.8
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Haghverdi00b"} Haghverdi 00b, section 6
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+categories"} compact closed categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            free on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traced+monoidal+category"} traced monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            (as discussed there). In these references therefore the notation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(-)
            from (
            %a{:href => "#JoyalStreetVerity96"}> Joyal-Street-Verity 96
            ) is changed to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}(-)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#AbramskyHaghverdiScott02"} Abramsky-Haghverdi-Scott 02, p. 11
          %p
            The objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            are pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+, A^-)
            of objects of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+ , A^-) \to (B^+ , B^-)
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            is given by a morphism in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi A
                            %mo +
                          %mo ⊗
                          %msup
                            %mi B
                            %mo −
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi A
                            %mo −
                          %mo ⊗
                          %msup
                            %mi B
                            %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  A^+\otimes B^-
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  A^- \otimes B^+
                  }
          %p
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of two such morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+ , A^-) \to (B^+ , B^-)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B^+ , B^-) \to (C^+ , C^-)
            is given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} tracing out
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^-
            in the evident way.
          %p
            We observe now that subcategories of such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            are famous in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            as “categories of
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superoperators"} superoperators
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operations"} quantum operations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
            on spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operators"} operators
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            that takes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrices"} density matrices
            (hence mixed
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+positive+maps"} completely positive maps
          %p
            First notice that if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traced+monoidal+category"} traced monoidal category
            structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            happens to be that induced by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            structure, then by compact closure the morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^+ , A^-) \to (B^+ , B^-)
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            above are equivalently morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (A^-)^\ast \otimes A^+ \longrightarrow (B^-)^\ast \otimes B^+
          %p
            and if moreover one concentrates on the case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \coloneqq A^- \simeq A^+
            etc. then these are morphisms of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  End(A) \longrightarrow End(B)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Under this identification the somewhat curious-looking composition in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            given by tracing in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            becomes just plain composition in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            Subcategories (non-full) of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
            consisting of morphisms of this form in some ambient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            have been considered in (
            %a{:href => "#Selinger05"}> Selinger 05
            ), there denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CPM
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CPM(\mathcal{C})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operations"} quantum operations
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CoeckeHeunen11"} Coecke-Heunen 11, section 2
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#AbramskyCoecke02"} Abramsky-Coecke 02
          %p
            Now the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CPM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CPM(-)
            construction requires and assumes that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            has all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} dual objects
            (which in the standard model means to restrict to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            with are of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite"} finite
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is given in (
            %a{:href => "#CoeckeHeunen11"}> Coecke-Heunen 11, def. 1
            ), there denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CP
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CP(\mathcal{C})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CP
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CP(\mathcal{C})
            is still a (non-full) subcategory of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Int(\mathcal{C})
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transcendental+syntax"} transcendental syntax
          %h2#references References
          %p The original references are
          %ul#Girard89
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"}> Jean-Yves Girard
              \. Towards a geometry of interaction. In Categories in Computer Science and Logic, pages 69 – 108, Providence, 1989. American Mathematical Society. Proceedings of Symposia in Pure Mathematics n92.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"}> Jean-Yves Girard
                \. Geometry of interaction I: interpretation of system F. In Ferro, Bonotto, Valentini, and Zanardo, editors, Logic Colloquium ‘88, pages 221 – 260, Amsterdam, 1989. North-Holland.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"}> Jean-Yves Girard
                \. Geometry of interaction II : deadlock-free algorithms. In Martin-Löf and Mints, editors, Proceedings of COLOG 88, volume 417 of Lecture Notes in Computer Science, pages 76 – 93, Heidelberg, 1990. Springer-Verlag.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"}> Jean-Yves Girard
                \. Geometry of interaction III : accommodating the additives. In Girard, Lafont, and Regnier, editors, Advances in Linear Logic, pages 329 – 389, Cambridge, 1995. Cambridge University Press.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Yves+Girard"}> Jean-Yves Girard
